WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.742
SQL has rules for deciding which logical operators are evaluated first.

00:00:04.742 --> 00:00:11.797
In PostgreSQL, NOT has higher precedence than AND, and AND has higher precedence than OR.

00:00:11.797 --> 00:00:16.612
That means an expression like A OR B AND C is interpreted as A OR the combination of

00:00:16.612 --> 00:00:24.462
B AND C. SQL does not automatically read it the way a human might casually read the sentence.

00:00:24.462 --> 00:00:28.202
You could memorize that order, and it is useful to know it,

00:00:28.202 --> 00:00:32.317
but I do not recommend relying on memory when writing important logic.

00:00:32.317 --> 00:00:34.392
Parentheses are much safer.

00:00:34.392 --> 00:00:41.372
Suppose a scholarship rule says a student qualifies when the student has a GPA above 3.5 OR belongs

00:00:41.372 --> 00:00:47.027
to a special program, AND the student has submitted the required form.

00:00:47.027 --> 00:00:54.147
If we do not group the conditions, the form requirement may apply only to the special-program branch.

00:00:54.147 --> 00:00:58.707
Students with high GPA could qualify even without the form.

00:00:58.707 --> 00:01:02.017
That may be exactly what the policy intends, or it may be completely wrong.

00:01:02.017 --> 00:01:04.382
Parentheses make the decision explicit.

00:01:04.382 --> 00:01:11.462
They also help the next analyst who reads the query months later.

00:01:11.462 --> 00:01:14.212
Think of parentheses as documentation, not just syntax.

00:01:14.212 --> 00:01:18.957
My recommendation for this course is simple: whenever AND and OR appear in the same rule,

00:01:18.957 --> 00:01:26.192
group the conditions in a way that mirrors the plain-language requirement.

00:01:26.192 --> 00:01:31.338
You should not need to mentally calculate precedence just to understand a business rule.
